I was thinking about this the other day.

I've never attempted to study the NetHack source, and I dare say that a wall 
of C wouldn't immediately give much of an idea of the map-generating 
algorithm, but the maps are very consistent, and you definitely do get a 
sense after a couple of hundred games of where the doors are going to be. 
You even start to know what's going on when two corridors overlap and 
there's a funny little corridor-corner -- you think, oh yeah, so the door 
must be *right here*, and you search, and presto.

So anyway I started thinking about the possibility of trying to work out the 
algorithm (or emulate it closely) based only on that gradual "how dungeons 
are laid out" feeling. Like a lot of things in life (e.g. how to walk --  
you'd have trouble describing exactly what your legs do), it seems like 
something that's trivial in theory but barely possible in practice.

